  "question": {
    "text": "<p>One mole of an ideal gas expands adiabatically from an initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math> to final state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math>. Another mole of the same gas expands isothermally from a different initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math> to the same final state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math>. The ratio of the specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume of this ideal gas is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#947;</mi></math>. What is the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mfrac></math>?</p>",
    "image": null
  },
